language ESSENCE' 1.0
$ csplib_009_perfect_square_placement: an Essence' model from DCP Rosetta.
$
$ The perfect square placement problem (also called the squared square problem) is to pack a set of squares with given integer sizes into a bigger square, so that no squares overlap each other and all square borders are parallel to the border of the big square. The sum of the square surfaces is equal to the surface of the packing square, so that there is no spare capacity. Find the coordinates of each square, counting from 0.
$
$ Save this file as model.eprime. Download `csplib_009_perfect_square_placement.json` and `dcp_convert_essence.py` from the
$ problem's page:
$   https://dcp-bench.github.io/DCP-Rosetta/problems/csplib_009_perfect_square_placement.html
$ Convert the instances once, then run the first one with Savile Row 1.11 (it needs Java); the
$ solution is written to `csplib_009_perfect_square_placement_1.param.solution`:
$   python dcp_convert_essence.py csplib_009_perfect_square_placement.json
$   savilerow model.eprime csplib_009_perfect_square_placement_1.param -run-solver

$ base: the side length of the big square
given base : int(1..)
$ sides_dim1: the number of squares (derived from the data)
given sides_dim1 : int(1..)
$ sides[i]: the side length of square i (indices 1-based)
given sides : matrix indexed by [int(1..sides_dim1)] of int(1..)

$ x_coords[i]: the column of the top-left corner of square i, counting from 0 (values 0 to base-1, reported as they are)
find x_coords : matrix indexed by [int(1..sides_dim1)] of int(0..base-1)
$ y_coords[i]: the row of the top-left corner of square i, counting from 0
find y_coords : matrix indexed by [int(1..sides_dim1)] of int(0..base-1)

such that
$ each square lies inside the big square, along both directions
forAll i : int(1..sides_dim1) . (x_coords[i] + sides[i] <= base /\ y_coords[i] + sides[i] <= base),
$ no two squares overlap: for each pair of squares, one lies to the left of, right of, above or below the other
forAll i : int(1..sides_dim1) . forAll j : int(1..sides_dim1) . (i < j) -> ((x_coords[i] + sides[i] <= x_coords[j]) \/ (x_coords[j] + sides[j] <= x_coords[i]) \/ (y_coords[i] + sides[i] <= y_coords[j]) \/ (y_coords[j] + sides[j] <= y_coords[i])),
$ the squares fill the big square exactly: the sum of their areas is the area of the big square
(sum i : int(1..sides_dim1) . sides[i] * sides[i]) = base * base
